---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Spine from '@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Spine.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const line = [
  // LIVING is declared, not inferred. See the note below the array.
  ['Derrick', '', 'Named here by his own permission, and by name only.', 'doc', 'LIVING'],
  ['LEONARD AUBREY ROBERT WEAR', 'b. 1917 Hitchin, d. 1973 or 1979',
   'Birth registered Hitchin 1917. On the 1921 census at 72 Pix Road, Letchworth, aged four. Sailed alone from Southampton on 12 March 1937, aged twenty. Married at Johannesburg in 1940 and again in 1951. Corporal 141272, Union Defence Force, a prisoner of war in Germany 1943 to 1945. Bought his father’s grave at West Park on 13 September 1969, of 271 Wilson Street, Fairlands.', 'doc'],
  ['HENRY ROBERT WEAR × MARTHA MAUD CASTLE', '1889–1969 · 1895–1986',
   'Married at the CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, BALDOCK on SATURDAY 31 OCTOBER 1914, registered Hitchin Q4 1914. He was born at St Olave Bermondsey in 1889, a printer’s assistant at Letchworth in 1911 and a machine minder at J. M. Dent & Sons by 1921. Sailed to the Cape on the Durban Castle, 5 April 1939; she followed on the Dunvegan Castle to Durban on 10 August with the four youngest children. Both buried in grave 56045, West Park, Johannesburg.', 'doc'],
  ['ROBERT WEAR × EMMA HAWKINS', '1859–1890 · b. 1859',
   'He died at thirty-one in 1890 and she took the children home to her brother’s house at Watford, where the 1891 census finds them.', 'doc'],
  ['WILLIAM WEAR × SARAH BLOOM', 'b. c.1823 Newington · b. c.1822 Deptford',
   'He was baptised at Newington on 10 April 1833, aged ten, with three of his brothers and sisters. His parents were JOHN WEAR and ELIZABETH. He was a sadler at Bermondsey in 1851 and a harness maker in 1871; she was born at Deptford in Kent, which the 1871 census gives as Bermondsey.', 'doc'],
  ['JOHN WEAR × ELIZABETH', 'of Newington, Surrey · children b. 1820–1827',
   'NEW, 27 September 2026, and held as probable rather than proved. Four of their children were baptised together at Newington on 10 April 1833 — Matilda 1820, William 1823, Thomas 1825, Mary Ann 1827 — and a pair baptised in 1819 to a father John in the same parish may be the same couple. It is an index of the register, not the register, and it carries no occupation, so the trade test this archive nominated has not been run.', 'inf'],
];

/* THE SAME LINE, ONTO THE SHARED SPINE — 21 September 2026.
   Six other archives draw their direct line with the kit's Spine: a numbered
   generation, the couple, the dates, and a mark on any joint that cannot bear
   weight. This one drew a three-column table, which is why it looked like no
   other archive in the estate.
   The rows below are not retyped. The array above is left exactly as it was
   written and mapped here, so no prose passes through a transcription. What
   the mapping adds is what the table had no column for: the husband and wife
   as separate people, and the living rule applied by the component rather
   than by a sentence.

   *** AND THE LIVING FLAG IS DECLARED RATHER THAN INFERRED, WHICH IT WAS NOT. ***
   The conversion first derived it as `living = !dates` -- no dates, therefore
   alive. That is true of this array today and it is NOT A RULE, it is a
   reading of a shape. A generation with a name and no dates for any other
   reason -- a couple at the top of the line whose years are simply not known,
   which is exactly what happens as a line is pushed back -- would be marked
   living and be wrong. THE ONE CONSTRAINT ON THIS SITE THAT MUST NOT FAIL
   SHOULD NOT REST ON AN ABSENCE. A fifth element 'LIVING' now says so
   explicitly, and a row with no dates and no flag is treated as dead with
   dates unknown, which is the safe direction to be wrong in: it withholds a
   guard from nobody who has one, and it never invents a birth year.
   The peer session that made the conversion named this weakness itself. */
const spine = line.map(([who, dates, how, conf, flag], i) => {
  const [self, wife] = who.split(' \u00d7 ');
  const [hisLife, herLife] = (dates || '').split(' \u00b7 ');
  const living = flag === 'LIVING';
  let born = null, died = null;
  if (!living && hisLife) {
    const bd = hisLife.match(/^b\.\s*(.+?),\s*d\.\s*(.+)$/);
    const dash = hisLife.match(/^(.+?)[\u2013\u2014](.+)$/);
    if (bd) { born = bd[1]; died = bd[2]; }
    else if (dash) { born = dash[1].trim(); died = dash[2].trim(); }
    else born = hisLife.replace(/^b\.\s*/, '');
  }
  return {
    gen: i,
    name: self,
    spouse: wife ? (herLife ? wife + ' (' + herLife + ')' : wife) : null,
    born, died, living,
    note: how,
    confidence: living ? null : (conf === 'doc' ? 'documented' : conf),
  };
});
const open = [
  ['a', 'WILLIAM WEAR’S OWN PARENTS',
   'He was born about 1823 at Newington, Surrey — before civil registration began in 1837. This is a parish-register problem, not an index one.',
   'NOW ANSWERABLE BY PURCHASE. See below: an 1842 marriage certificate names the fathers of both parties.'],
  ['b', 'THE WILLIAM WEAR × SARAH BLOOM MARRIAGE',
   'Located 20 September 2026: registered QUARTER 3 of 1842 at STEPNEY, volume 2, page 430.',
   'ONE-IN-FOUR, NOT PROVED. Before 1912 the index does not name the spouse. Four people are printed on that page: WILLIAM WEARE, Thomas Moyes, William George Smith, James Wicks.'],
  ['c', 'SARAH BLOOM’S FATHER, AND THE YARMOUTH BRANCH',
   'A niece, Sarah Ann Bloom, seventeen and born at Yarmouth, is in the 1871 household at Jamaica Road. Her birth is registered Yarmouth Q3 1853, 4B/1, mother’s maiden name MORT.',
   'THE SIDEWAYS ROUTE IS SHUT. The whole England and Wales index holds five MORT marriages in Norfolk, ever, and the earliest is 1885 — thirty-two years too late. The brother married elsewhere.'],
  ['d', 'LEONARD’S DEATH — 1973 OR 1979',
   'Six instruments have looked and none can reach him.',
   'ONE PLACE LEFT: the South African death register at the Department of Home Affairs. FamilySearch’s South African civil death index stops in 1966; the Transvaal estate-file death notices are published for 1955–1960 only.'],
  ['e', 'A LIVING MALE-LINE WEAR, FOR A Y TEST',
   'Derrick’s Y haplogroup is R-S11477 — R1b, branch U106/Z381, a North Sea lineage. Re-checked 20 September 2026: of 7,755 public members of the R-U106 project, 34 sit on his branch, and there is STILL NO WEAR, WEIR OR WARE anywhere on it.',
   'The surname projects on the branch are Ballew, Stafford, Blakely, Byrd and York — unchanged since 14 September. A dated negative, re-dated.'],
];
---
<Base title="Where the Wear line stands"
  subtitle="Five generations, and the four questions above them"
  lede="A stocktake rather than an argument. What the line is, how each step is held up, and — the part that matters — exactly what is still open at the top of it and what would close each one.">

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card stat"><b>5</b><span>Generations documented</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>1842</b><span>Earliest record located</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>1</b><span>Certificate that would answer two questions</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>0</b><span>Wears on the Y branch</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>The line</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    Read downwards. Every step below rests on a document somebody opened, not on a family tree.
  </p>
  <Spine rows={spine} u={(p) => base + p} archiveKey="luwinski" />

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>One certificate would answer two of these <span class="tag inf">20 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Stepney, quarter 3 of 1842, volume 2, page 430.</strong> Sarah Bloom&rsquo;s marriage is
      registered on that page, and so are three other brides; the four grooms printed with them
      include a <strong>William Weare</strong>. The index of that period does not say who married whom.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>An 1842 marriage certificate names both fathers, with their occupations.</strong> So
      ordering that one page would give <em>William Wear&rsquo;s father</em> &mdash; question (a), the
      brick wall at the top of this line &mdash; and <em>Sarah Bloom&rsquo;s father</em> &mdash;
      question (c), the whole point of the Yarmouth enquiry. It would also settle question (b) by
      saying plainly whether these two are the pair on that page.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      It is a purchase from the General Register Office, so it is not this archive&rsquo;s to make.
      It is the single highest-value order on the whole work list.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" id="wedding1914">
    <h3>The wedding, from two papers that had never been opened
      <span class="tag doc">27 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      For a fortnight this archive has carried the marriage as an index line: <em>Castle&ndash;Wear,
      Hitchin, quarter 4 of 1914, volume 3A page 1552</em> — the only Wear&ndash;Castle marriage in
      the whole English index, and a three-month window. <strong>Findmypast&rsquo;s newspaper archive
      was paid for on 16 September and never once searched.</strong> It holds two reports of the day.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      Military Wedding. There was a military wedding at the Congregational Church last Saturday. The
      bridegroom, Mr. <strong>Henry Robert Wear</strong>, of Letchworth, who was in full uniform, was
      married to Miss <strong>Martha Maude Castle</strong> (of Baldock). The Rev. T. Smith officiated.
      <span class="small">— <em>Citizen</em> (Letchworth), Friday 6 November 1914, page 4</span>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Soldier&rsquo;s Wedding.</strong> — At the Congregational Church on Saturday the wedding
      took place quietly of Miss <strong>Maude Castle, of Norton Street</strong>, and Mr.
      <strong>Harry Ware</strong>, of Letchworth. The bridegroom was on furlough from Bury St. Edmunds,
      the headquarters of the <strong>1st Herts Territorials</strong>, to which he is attached. The
      Rev. T. Smith, pastor, officiated, and after a short stay the bridegroom rejoined his regiment,
      which later received orders to proceed to the Front.
      <span class="small">— <em>Hertfordshire Express</em>, Saturday 7 November 1914, page 4, under
      <strong>Baldock</strong></span>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      One says &ldquo;last Saturday&rdquo; on a Friday, the other &ldquo;on Saturday&rdquo; on the
      Saturday after. <strong>Both resolve to 31 October 1914</strong>, and it was a Saturday.
      Four things follow that the index could not give:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The church.</strong> The Congregational chapel at Baldock, where the Rev. T. Smith
        was pastor — not the parish church and not a registry office. The Nonconformity is a fact
        about the family, not only about the building.</li>
      <li><strong>Her street.</strong> <strong>Norton Street, Baldock</strong> — the first address this
        archive has ever had for Martha Maud Castle before her marriage. Norton Street is in Baldock;
        it is not the neighbouring village of Norton.</li>
      <li><strong>He married on leave.</strong> Home on furlough from Bury St Edmunds, married quietly,
        rejoined — and the 1/1st Hertfordshires landed in France on <strong>6 November</strong>, the
        day the <em>Citizen</em> printed its notice. <strong>He had about a week.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>He is &ldquo;Harry Ware&rdquo; in one of them.</strong> A searcher looking for WEAR
        in these papers does not find him. The six spellings of Ivone&rsquo;s name have an English
        counterpart.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>The regiment was not news and that is the point.</strong> His WO 363 service record,
      number 3010, already put him in the 1st Battalion Hertfordshire Regiment. A service file and a
      village paper, written for different reasons by people who never met, place the same man in the
      same regiment in the same autumn. <strong>That is what corroboration looks like</strong>, and
      this archive has spent a week learning to want it.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Findmypast British Newspaper Archive, read 27 September 2026 on David&rsquo;s subscription.
      Texts are the publishers&rsquo; corrected OCR of 1914 printing, which is out of copyright; the
      scanned images are not reproduced here.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" id="newington1833">
    <h3>Four children at the font on one day, and a generation above the line
      <span class="tag inf">Probable, 27 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      William Wear the saddler has stood at the top of this line with no parents, because he was born
      about <strong>1823 at Newington in Surrey</strong> and civil registration began in 1837 &mdash;
      fourteen years too late to name his father. The work list nominated two levers: the 1841 census,
      and the Newington baptism register.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The census gave nothing.</strong> Eight William Wears of the right age in Surrey in 1841
      &mdash; Clapham, Blechingley, Dorking, Croydon and four at Lambeth &mdash; and not one at
      Newington or Walworth. At eighteen he may well have been living in as an apprentice rather than
      under his father&rsquo;s roof.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The baptism register answered.</strong> William Wear, born 1823, baptised at Newington
      on <strong>10 April 1833</strong>, father <strong>John Wear</strong>, mother
      <strong>Elizabeth</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A ten-year gap between birth and baptism is odd enough to check, and the check explains it.
      <strong>Four children of John Wear were baptised at Newington on that same day</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Child</th><th>Born</th><th>Baptised</th><th>Father</th><th>Mother</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Matilda</strong></td><td>1820</td><td>10 Apr 1833</td><td>John Wear</td><td>Elizabeth</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>William</strong></td><td>1823</td><td>10 Apr 1833</td><td>John Wear</td><td>Elizabeth</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Thomas</strong></td><td>1825</td><td>10 Apr 1833</td><td>John Wear</td><td>Elizabeth</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mary Ann</strong></td><td>1827</td><td>10 Apr 1833</td><td>John Wear</td><td><span class="muted">not opened</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      A family bringing its children to the font together, the eldest already thirteen, with births
      spaced two to three years apart. <strong>Matilda and Thomas were opened in full and both give
      the mother as Elizabeth</strong>, which is the test that matters: this is a household, not one
      index line that happens to match.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The same search returns <strong>Elizabeth Wear born 1812 and John Wear born 1817, baptised
      together in 1819</strong> to a father John in the same parish. If that is the same couple, the
      family runs Elizabeth, John, Matilda, William, Thomas, Mary Ann, and the eldest daughter carries
      her mother&rsquo;s name &mdash; which is the ordinary practice. <strong>Not asserted:</strong>
      those two were not opened, and &ldquo;father John&rdquo; is not an identification.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>Three things this is not.</strong> It is an <strong>index of the register and not the
      register</strong> &mdash; a FamilySearch transcription &mdash; so it carries no occupation and
      no abode. <strong>The trade is exactly what is missing</strong>, and the trade was the lever this
      archive nominated: harness making ran in this family, and a Newington saddler with a son William
      would be a narrow target. And <strong>Newington had more than one Wear household</strong>: the
      same search turns up a John Wear baptised 1825 to a <em>John Southey Wear</em>. In a parish with
      two Wear families a forename match is not an identification, which is why the sibling group
      carries this and the single line does not.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      It also does not make the certificate unnecessary. William John Wear&rsquo;s marriage of Q2 1871
      at St Olave Southwark, vol 1D p.269, has a father&rsquo;s name and occupation column filled in
      by somebody who knew him, and would confirm this from the other end in a document rather than an
      index.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What is still open above the line</h2>
  {open.map(([k, what, state, verdict]) => (
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>({k}) <span set:html={what} /></h3>
      <p class="small">{state}</p>
      <p class="small"><strong>{verdict}</strong></p>
    </div>
  ))}

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the other side of that generation</h3>
    <p class="small">
      Emma Hawkins was the daughter of <strong>JOHN HAWKINS</strong> b.1820 and
      <strong>EMMA HAWKINS</strong> b.1822, of <strong>The Red Lion Cottages, Aldenham</strong>,
      Hertfordshire &mdash; farm labourers, where the Wears and the Blooms are London tradesmen. The two
      halves of this generation could hardly be less alike, and both are documented from the 1871 census.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      A warning kept in place deliberately: there are <strong>two Emma Hawkinses</strong>, mother and
      daughter, born about 1822 and in 1859. This archive once merged them and published the result.
      See <a href={base + '/method/'}>how corrections work</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
